WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:04.520
Is Robert Pattinson the best actor to play
the Caped Crusader in live action?

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:08.450
Does Kevin Conroy still reign supreme when
it comes to Batman voice actors?

00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:11.389
And where does Adam West's droll straight
man figure into it all?

00:00:11.389 --> 00:00:13.961
These are the movie versions of Batman ranked
worst to best.

00:00:13.961 --> 00:00:19.330
The thing you really have to understand about
the original Batman serials is that they're

00:00:19.330 --> 00:00:20.330
bad.

00:00:20.330 --> 00:00:23.900
Not just bad in retrospect after 70 years
of advancements in filmmaking and special

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:26.859
effects, although they certainly fit that
bill pretty handily.

00:00:26.859 --> 00:00:28.869
No, they're just bad.

00:00:28.869 --> 00:00:32.770
The first serial, released by Columbia in
15 parts, amounts to four and a half hours

00:00:32.770 --> 00:00:36.070
of the worst version of Batman to ever hit
the silver screen.

00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:40.540
Batman and Robin, played by Lewis Wilson and
Douglas Croft, are recast as government agents

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:44.320
who joined up to help the war effort and are
tasked with stopping the villainous Dr. Daka

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:45.770
from building a death ray.

00:00:45.770 --> 00:00:47.930
In theory, that's a pretty exciting plot.

00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:50.660
Unfortunately, the actual execution is atrocious.

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:55.160
It somehow winds up being repetitive even
by the standards of serials, with alarmingly

00:00:55.160 --> 00:01:00.660
bad special effects, hilariously awkward costumes,
unconvincing fights, and, of course, a massive

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:02.970
helping of racism to bring it all together.

00:01:02.970 --> 00:01:05.800
Overall, this is a Batman best forgotten.

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:11.540
The best thing you can say about 1949's "Batman
and Robin" is that it's not as racist as its

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:12.540
predecessor.

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:14.710
Unfortunately, that's about all you can say.

00:01:14.710 --> 00:01:19.010
Robert Lowery's Batman stumbles from one goofy
cliffhanger to the next in an attempt to unmask

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:20.930
a mad scientist called the Wizard.

00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:24.540
Four hours later, it turns out to be exactly
who you thought he was.

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:28.790
We'll take "hilariously silly" over "upsettingly
racist" any day of the week, but the fact

00:01:28.790 --> 00:01:33.360
remains that Lowery's bumbling, barely present
Batman is trapped in a pretty awful plot that

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:35.560
stretches out even longer than the first serial.

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:40.060
The worst part, though, is the costume: a
straight up sweatsuit capped off with an ill-fitting

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:41.909
cowl.

00:01:41.909 --> 00:01:45.740
Every now and then, an idea comes along that
seems like an absolute no-brainer.

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:49.570
Such is the case with the casting of Peter
Weller as the older Batman drawn out of retirement

00:01:49.570 --> 00:01:53.299
in the animated adaptation of Frank Miller's
groundbreaking "Dark Knight Returns."

00:01:53.299 --> 00:01:55.440
It sounds so good on paper, right?

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:59.841
An older, more brutal, and even angrier Batman,
played by the guy who brought his voice to

00:01:59.841 --> 00:02:01.130
the title role of "RoboCop."

00:02:01.130 --> 00:02:05.520
It's such a perfect idea that it's hard to
argue that they should've done anything else.

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:08.789
Unfortunately, the idea is the only part that's
perfect.

00:02:08.789 --> 00:02:12.510
Whether it's Weller's unfamiliarity with the
art of voice acting or the desired effect

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:16.370
from director Jay Oliva, Weller sounds in
every scene like he just woke up.

00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:19.010
He might actually be the worst part of the
film.

00:02:19.010 --> 00:02:23.590
Rather than a dynamic growl that goes with
the action, Weller winds up just being boring,

00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:27.180
and whatever problems you might have with
The Dark Knight Returns, "boring" should not

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:28.180
be one of them.

00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:29.680
"You are slow."

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:30.680
"True.

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:34.519
But we're all slow when we're thigh deep in
mud."

00:02:34.519 --> 00:02:38.919
Bruce Greenwood can best be described as the
2003 Toyota Corolla of Batman actors.

00:02:38.919 --> 00:02:42.980
Nothing fancy, no real features to speak of,
and it doesn't quite have the appeal of the

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:46.919
older classic models or newer, better ones,
but it'll get you where you're going.

00:02:46.919 --> 00:02:50.939
Okay, so maybe that's a harsh way of putting
it, but it's pretty accurate.

00:02:50.939 --> 00:02:55.790
Greenwood has played Batman on multiple occasions
— three DTV movies, a videogame and the

00:02:55.790 --> 00:03:00.159
"Young Justice" TV show — and it's probably
telling that there's only one of those, the

00:03:00.159 --> 00:03:02.980
adaptation of "Gotham by Gaslight," where
he's the focus.

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:06.980
Even then, the emphasis is less on Batman
as a character than it is on the idea of seeing

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:10.859
all the familiar characters recast in the
Victorian era setting of a story in which

00:03:10.859 --> 00:03:12.730
Batman fights Jack the Ripper.

00:03:12.730 --> 00:03:16.920
The others are all focused on different characters,
and in the case of "Under the Red Hood," Greenwood

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:21.099
is playing Batman opposite "Supernatural"
star Jensen Ackles, whose performance as Jason

00:03:21.099 --> 00:03:24.150
Todd is so full of charisma that he quickly
upstages his co-star.

00:03:24.150 --> 00:03:26.610
"You don't understand.

00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:29.549
I don't think you've ever understood."

00:03:29.549 --> 00:03:31.029
"What?

00:03:31.029 --> 00:03:34.200
Your moral code just won't allow for that?"

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:38.989
Since actor Jensen Ackles did such a bang-up
job as Jason Todd in 2010's animated adaptation

00:03:38.989 --> 00:03:42.719
of "Under the Red Hood," he got his chance
as the Caped Crusader himself in a pair of

00:03:42.719 --> 00:03:45.549
"Long Halloween" adaptations in 2021.

00:03:45.549 --> 00:03:50.720
Unfortunately, Ackles showed his talents weren't
quite the fit for Batman that many had anticipated.

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:54.260
Ackles delivers a stock standard Wayne and
slightly gruffer Dark Knight;

00:03:54.260 --> 00:03:59.069
"Attacking us like that, out in the open…

00:03:59.069 --> 00:04:00.609
It didn't fit the profile."

00:04:00.609 --> 00:04:04.659
Troy Baker, ironically enough, becomes the
hero of this adaptation by giving an excellent

00:04:04.659 --> 00:04:06.829
Hamill impersonation as the Joker.

00:04:06.829 --> 00:04:11.670
David Dastmalchian likewise offers up a genuinely
chilling performance here as Calendar Man.

00:04:11.670 --> 00:04:16.060
This arc features most of Batman's rogues
gallery appearing in one way or another, each

00:04:16.060 --> 00:04:18.040
character playing their own small role.

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:21.780
If you're a Batman fan, it's a must-watch;
just don't expect Ackles' work here to give

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:24.639
you any new insight into the character.

00:04:24.639 --> 00:04:27.820
On paper, Ben Affleck seems like a pretty
solid choice for Batman.

00:04:27.820 --> 00:04:31.400
He's got those square-jawed good looks and
he's more than capable of bringing a deep

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:33.880
focus and intensity to roles when they call
for it.

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:37.710
In practice, though, the Batman of "Dawn of
Justice" is only saved from being the worst

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:41.790
superhero in his universe because Superman
is somehow even worse.

00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:45.319
It's worth noting that much of director Zack
Snyder's inspiration came from Frank Miller's

00:04:45.319 --> 00:04:47.590
landmark story "The Dark Knight Returns."

00:04:47.590 --> 00:04:51.780
Unfortunately, while the movie recreated entire
scenes wholesale from the comics, they're

00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:54.289
all divorced from their original context.

00:04:54.289 --> 00:04:58.490
Affleck's Batman is a ludicrously violent
murderer who's easily manipulated into a fistfight

00:04:58.490 --> 00:05:02.450
with another superhero—a fistfight that
ends when the person he's fighting reminds

00:05:02.450 --> 00:05:04.900
him that other people also have moms.

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:08.130
You'd think that would be something he would've
learned in his years as the World's Greatest

00:05:08.130 --> 00:05:09.130
Detective.

00:05:09.130 --> 00:05:11.020
"What does that mean?

00:05:11.020 --> 00:05:14.509
Why did you say that name?"

00:05:14.509 --> 00:05:18.580
The animated adaptation of Frank Miller and
Dave Mazzucchelli's "Batman: Year One" fares

00:05:18.580 --> 00:05:22.490
a little better than "The Dark Knight Returns"
for a few reasons, but the casting of its

00:05:22.490 --> 00:05:24.460
title character was not one of them.

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:28.080
Ben McKenzie approaches the role of a young
Bruce Wayne trying to figure out how best

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:32.490
to fight crime with what can only be described
as the monotone delivery of a sexy robot.

00:05:32.490 --> 00:05:36.380
Seriously, we've tried to think of other ways
to describe it, but that is unquestionably

00:05:36.380 --> 00:05:37.660
the best one.

00:05:37.660 --> 00:05:39.689
"You've eaten well.

00:05:39.689 --> 00:05:45.669
You've eaten Gotham's wealth, its spirit,
but your feast is nearly over."

00:05:45.669 --> 00:05:49.479
All of the intensity and emotion of key scenes
from the original, like being inspired by

00:05:49.479 --> 00:05:53.530
the bat crashing through his window or announcing
his presence to Gotham's wealthy white-collar

00:05:53.530 --> 00:05:57.699
criminals, is lost in favor of the sensual
monotony of some kind of pleasure droid.

00:05:57.699 --> 00:06:01.699
Once you have that image in your head, it
does make McKenzie's Batman a little more

00:06:01.699 --> 00:06:04.189
interesting, but it doesn't rescue the performance.

00:06:04.189 --> 00:06:08.740
Instead, ironically enough, Batman winds up
being overshadowed by another "Year One" main

00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:12.449
character, Jim Gordon, who's played here by
Bryan Cranston in a role that McKenzie would

00:06:12.449 --> 00:06:14.820
get a few years later as the star of "Gotham."

00:06:14.820 --> 00:06:18.849
"It's been years since I had to take out a
green beret.

00:06:18.849 --> 00:06:21.650
Even so, he deserves a handicap."

00:06:21.650 --> 00:06:26.729
"Crisis on Two Earths" was one of the earlier
entries into the ever-expanding roster of

00:06:26.729 --> 00:06:31.159
DC Universe Animated Original Movies, and
generally speaking, it's also one of the best.

00:06:31.159 --> 00:06:35.570
It was loosely based on some classic JLA stories,
giving it a solid foundation.

00:06:35.570 --> 00:06:39.650
It was written by the late Dwayne McDuffie,
who was the story editor for "Justice League

00:06:39.650 --> 00:06:40.650
Unlimited."

00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:44.099
It even has some great fight scenes and solid
visuals that are a step above what viewers

00:06:44.099 --> 00:06:45.099
would get later.

00:06:45.099 --> 00:06:47.689
In fact, pretty much everything about the
movie is great...

00:06:47.689 --> 00:06:48.689
except Batman.

00:06:48.689 --> 00:06:52.930
It was a weird bit of stunt casting to get
William Baldwin to play the Dark Knight, but

00:06:52.930 --> 00:06:54.499
he's not really bad in the part.

00:06:54.499 --> 00:06:58.599
He just never really sounds like Batman, in
a way that's difficult to pin down.

00:06:58.599 --> 00:07:02.560
"What was so valuable that the Crime Syndicate
would cross dimensions to get it?"

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:03.909
"Did you keep it from her?"

00:07:03.909 --> 00:07:05.979
"Her henchman escaped with it.

00:07:05.979 --> 00:07:07.110
Whatever it was."

00:07:07.110 --> 00:07:10.540
Maybe it's just that this feels like a lost
episode of "Justice League," which makes it

00:07:10.540 --> 00:07:13.970
a little weird that it's not Kevin Conroy's
voice coming out of Batman.

00:07:13.970 --> 00:07:18.270
Maybe it's that this appears to be Baldwin's
very first voice acting gig, giving him some

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:21.189
intangible quality that sounds a little off.

00:07:21.189 --> 00:07:23.710
Whatever it is, it's just not quite right.

00:07:23.710 --> 00:07:28.860
2021's "Soul of the Dragon" is another modern
DC animated outing that recasts the Dark Knight,

00:07:28.860 --> 00:07:32.569
and it has a lot of good things going for
it — even if the least impressive of them

00:07:32.569 --> 00:07:33.979
is its take on Batman.

00:07:33.979 --> 00:07:37.900
"Grimm" actor David Giuntoli gives a dry performance
as Bruce Wayne.

00:07:37.900 --> 00:07:42.069
His Batman doesn't fare much less grim in
the rare instances he dons the cape at all;

00:07:42.069 --> 00:07:46.460
this movie focuses as much on Mark Dacascos
doing his best Bruce Lee impression as Richard

00:07:46.460 --> 00:07:48.490
Dragon, as it does on Bruce Wayne.

00:07:48.490 --> 00:07:50.879
"We're in this together, Bruce.

00:07:50.879 --> 00:07:52.080
Just like we've always been.

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:54.610
Like we're supposed to be.

00:07:54.610 --> 00:07:58.139
We are the dragon."

00:07:58.139 --> 00:08:03.050
This original Batman story was written by
"Animated Series" veteran Bruce Timm.

00:08:03.050 --> 00:08:06.789
Set in the 1970s, "Soul of the Dragon" is
an R-rated-like take on martial arts films,

00:08:06.789 --> 00:08:09.390
inspired largely by the Bruce Lee classic
"Enter the Dragon."

00:08:09.390 --> 00:08:13.569
Of course, it takes cues from the martial
arts-heavy "Batman Begins" as well.

00:08:13.569 --> 00:08:18.210
Overall, though, "Soul of the Dragon" is a
pretty good animated homage, if not necessarily

00:08:18.210 --> 00:08:19.930
a great Batman movie.

00:08:19.930 --> 00:08:22.479
The best way to describe "Agents of S.H.I.E.L.D."

00:08:22.479 --> 00:08:26.669
actor Jason O'Mara's take on Batman is this:
What would it sound like if George Clooney

00:08:26.669 --> 00:08:29.550
had been allowed to be Batman in a much less
campy setting?

00:08:29.550 --> 00:08:34.130
O'Mara's tone of voice is remarkably similar
to how Clooney sounded behind the cowl, even

00:08:34.130 --> 00:08:37.120
if he isn't allowed to embrace his charisma
as fully as Clooney could.

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:41.000
The story of "Hush" is a strong one, as Batman
must uncover the identity of a mysterious

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:42.400
new killer in Gotham.

00:08:42.400 --> 00:08:47.251
However, O'Mara delivers a confident, only
slightly brooding, take on the detective.

00:08:47.251 --> 00:08:53.260
"The events of the past months reach as high
as Superman and as low as the Joker.

00:08:53.260 --> 00:08:55.730
All were set into motion by Hush."

00:08:55.730 --> 00:09:00.520
O'Mara would came back for 2020's solid "Justice
League Dark: Apokolips War," where he reprised

00:09:00.520 --> 00:09:05.090
his role alongside a star-studded cast in
a very different, very violent sort of superhero

00:09:05.090 --> 00:09:06.090
story.

00:09:06.090 --> 00:09:10.090
But unfortunately, O'Mara's second shot at
the character wasn't enough to put him any

00:09:10.090 --> 00:09:11.380
higher on this list.

00:09:11.380 --> 00:09:14.410
First things first: Diedrich Bader is actually
a really good Batman.

00:09:14.410 --> 00:09:17.640
In "Batman: The Brave and the Bold," he served
as a cheerless Batman.

00:09:17.640 --> 00:09:22.090
His take was as a straight man for a cast
of superheroic oddballs in a series of team-ups

00:09:22.090 --> 00:09:24.900
that spanned the entirety of the DC Universe.

00:09:24.900 --> 00:09:28.150
Even the viewers who were initially put off
by the show's inherent silliness were won

00:09:28.150 --> 00:09:31.600
over when the show veered into darkness with
"Chill of the Night."

00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:36.900
"So just like that he signed away their lives,
so casually.

00:09:36.900 --> 00:09:38.190
Without remorse.

00:09:38.190 --> 00:09:39.860
Without blinking a filthy eye."

00:09:39.860 --> 00:09:43.950
Here's the problem: there's no "Brave and
the Bold" movie to show off Bader's chops,

00:09:43.950 --> 00:09:47.070
although he does get second billing in a straight-to-video
Scooby-Doo movie.

00:09:47.070 --> 00:09:50.200
That just leaves "JLA Adventures: Trapped
In Time."

00:09:50.200 --> 00:09:54.210
The story revolves around the Justice League
having to go back in time to save baby Superman

00:09:54.210 --> 00:09:56.250
from being murdered by the Legion of Doom.

00:09:56.250 --> 00:10:00.310
At one point it involves them literally throwing
the indestructible Kryptonian baby around

00:10:00.310 --> 00:10:01.310
like a football.

00:10:01.310 --> 00:10:05.960
Overall, though, it's pretty forgettable outside
of some truly bad costume designs, and none

00:10:05.960 --> 00:10:09.840
of the things that made Bader's Batman work
are on display.

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:14.540
In the run-up to the release of 2019's "Joker,"
there was a lot commentary about how writer/director

00:10:14.540 --> 00:10:18.210
Todd Philips had said that his take on the
title character with Joaquin Phoenix "didn't

00:10:18.210 --> 00:10:20.250
follow anything from the comic books."

00:10:20.250 --> 00:10:23.910
With that being the case, it was probably
pretty embarrassing for him to realize that

00:10:23.910 --> 00:10:28.580
he accidentally put both the Joker and Bruce
Wayne, who is actually the comic book superhero

00:10:28.580 --> 00:10:29.630
Batman, into the film.

00:10:29.630 --> 00:10:33.811
Sure, technically, the little boy Bruce Wayne
doesn't suit up as a dark avenger of the night

00:10:33.811 --> 00:10:37.680
or perform any of the ninja tricks Batman
is known for, but we're still counting him

00:10:37.680 --> 00:10:38.680
for this list.

00:10:38.680 --> 00:10:42.650
In fact, Dante Pereira-Olsen gives us an interesting
baseline for Bat-performances.

00:10:42.650 --> 00:10:46.970
He definitely doesn't do anything wrong in
his portrayal of LI'l Batman, but that's mostly

00:10:46.970 --> 00:10:49.210
because he doesn't really do anything, period.

00:10:49.210 --> 00:10:52.920
His most memorable moment in the film comes
in a scene during which Bruce stands there

00:10:52.920 --> 00:10:57.800
silently while a grown man puts his fingers
into Bruce's mouth and makes him "smile."

00:10:57.800 --> 00:11:02.110
Pereira-Olson is perfectly fine for what he's
given to do, which is nothing, and the end

00:11:02.110 --> 00:11:07.190
result is that this is the Bat-performance
equivalent of pure water: a completely value-neutral

00:11:07.190 --> 00:11:10.210
baseline that we can use as our midpoint.

00:11:10.210 --> 00:11:14.620
In virtually any other film, casting Jimmy
Kimmel as Batman would be downright alarming

00:11:14.620 --> 00:11:18.220
for fans, provoking the kind of discourse
that would make Ben Affleck getting the part

00:11:18.220 --> 00:11:20.090
look like a brief but civil debate.

00:11:20.090 --> 00:11:21.580
In "Teen Titans Go!

00:11:21.580 --> 00:11:25.370
To the Movies," however, it's not even in
the top five most notable casting choices.

00:11:25.370 --> 00:11:29.710
The big one is almost certainly Nicolas Cage's
all-too-brief performance as Superman, both

00:11:29.710 --> 00:11:33.720
because Cage was initially set to play the
Man of Steel in Tim Burton's "Superman Lives"

00:11:33.720 --> 00:11:35.860
and because it's Nicolas Cage as Superman.

00:11:35.860 --> 00:11:39.340
"I knew you were goofsters, but using Lois,
and now this?

00:11:39.340 --> 00:11:41.900
It's too much!"

00:11:41.900 --> 00:11:44.640
Compared to that, Kimmel as Batman doesn't
seem all that notable.

00:11:44.640 --> 00:11:47.180
Or, to be honest, all that memorable.

00:11:47.180 --> 00:11:50.250
Despite Batman being at the center of one
of the film's most notable moments, Kimmel

00:11:50.250 --> 00:11:52.690
is ultimately very forgettable as Batman.

00:11:52.690 --> 00:11:55.780
It's that rare case of a movie that just has
too much going on.

00:11:55.780 --> 00:12:00.920
In a movie that's soaked in lime green neon
and hot pink leopard print, Val Kilmer's portrayal

00:12:00.920 --> 00:12:05.440
of the Caped Crusader is... well, the kindest
way to put it would be "reserved," but let's

00:12:05.440 --> 00:12:06.730
not mince words here.

00:12:06.730 --> 00:12:11.300
Kilmer, only two years removed from a charismatic,
scene-stealing turn as Doc Holliday in "Tombstone,"

00:12:11.300 --> 00:12:15.320
drifts through "Batman Forever" with the screen
presence of a cardboard cutout.

00:12:15.320 --> 00:12:16.700
"What's wrong?"

00:12:16.700 --> 00:12:20.250
To be fair, there was a good reason for him
to make that choice as an actor.

00:12:20.250 --> 00:12:24.370
Director Joel Schumacher, Tim Burton's hand-picked
successor, was clearly drawing his inspiration

00:12:24.370 --> 00:12:28.770
from the 1966 television show, and with Jim
Carrey and Tommy Lee Jones chewing their way

00:12:28.770 --> 00:12:33.100
through every available bit of scenery, replicating
Adam West's straight man so they'd have something

00:12:33.100 --> 00:12:36.240
to bounce off of probably seemed like the
best way to go.

00:12:36.240 --> 00:12:40.390
There are parts where it actually works, too—the
best scenes by far in "Batman Forever" are

00:12:40.390 --> 00:12:45.330
the ones in which Carrey's Edward Nygma tries
to dress, act, and sound like his idol, Kilmer's

00:12:45.330 --> 00:12:46.330
Bruce Wayne.

00:12:46.330 --> 00:12:49.400
For those, he's the perfect straight man for
the wildly over-the-top villains.

00:12:49.400 --> 00:12:54.180
When it comes to actually being Batman, however,
Kilmer's Adam West impression just doesn't

00:12:54.180 --> 00:12:58.790
land, whether it's battling against the villains,
giving Dick Grayson a home, or having a monumentally

00:12:58.790 --> 00:13:01.970
tepid romance with Nicole Kidman's Dr. Chase
Meridian.

00:13:01.970 --> 00:13:04.740
It just flat-out doesn't work.

00:13:04.740 --> 00:13:08.440
If you're not familiar with "Batman Unlimited,"
that's probably because it's the reverse of

00:13:08.440 --> 00:13:09.930
the way things usually work.

00:13:09.930 --> 00:13:13.930
Rather than starting out with a movie or cartoon
and then creating toys based on that, this

00:13:13.930 --> 00:13:18.140
one started out as an action figure line that
was created to keep kid-friendly Batman toys

00:13:18.140 --> 00:13:23.070
in stores during the more PG-13 era of the
Christopher Nolan and Zack Snyder films.

00:13:23.070 --> 00:13:27.240
Once the toys were out, a series of animated
shorts and a trio of direct-to-video movies

00:13:27.240 --> 00:13:31.380
were released, and if you're into the weirder
side of Batman, they're well worth watching.

00:13:31.380 --> 00:13:35.000
You might expect a movie that only exists
to support an action figure line to feature

00:13:35.000 --> 00:13:39.200
a series of incredibly toyetic costumes, but
the "Unlimited" movies go a step further by

00:13:39.200 --> 00:13:44.050
giving Batman a new sidekick in the form of
a robot wolf that turns into a motorcycle.They're

00:13:44.050 --> 00:13:47.280
not exactly what you'd call "good," but they
are awesome.

00:13:47.280 --> 00:13:50.640
"I am shooting a viral video here!"

00:13:50.640 --> 00:13:54.280
Batman is brought to life this time courtesy
of veteran voice actor Roger Craig Smith.

00:13:54.280 --> 00:13:58.410
It might seem counterintuitive to cast the
same guy who played Sonic the Hedgehog and

00:13:58.410 --> 00:14:02.160
Thomas from "Regular Show" as Batman, but
he turns in a solid performance.

00:14:02.160 --> 00:14:06.180
He definitely shows why he was brought back
to play the Caped Crusader in other projects,

00:14:06.180 --> 00:14:11.900
like the English dub of "Batman: Ninja" and
most recently, 2020's "Superman: Red Son."

00:14:11.900 --> 00:14:16.620
To say "Batman and Robin" has a bad reputation
is putting it about as mildly as you possibly

00:14:16.620 --> 00:14:17.620
could.

00:14:17.620 --> 00:14:21.200
Whether or not it deserves all the hate, it's
commonly regarded as one of the worst superhero

00:14:21.200 --> 00:14:25.370
movies of all time, with a bloated cast of
villains and sidekicks sprawling out into

00:14:25.370 --> 00:14:28.390
a plot that starts off silly and ends up verging
on ludicrous.

00:14:28.390 --> 00:14:38.140
"I hate to disappoint you, but… rubber lips
are immune to your charms."

00:14:38.140 --> 00:14:42.120
It's so bad that both Joel Schumacher and
George Clooney have apologized for it, and

00:14:42.120 --> 00:14:45.930
it was credited with nearly killing the franchise
until it returned with a darker direction

00:14:45.930 --> 00:14:47.300
under Christopher Nolan.

00:14:47.300 --> 00:14:51.320
The thing is, as controversial as it might
sound, Clooney's not actually that bad in

00:14:51.320 --> 00:14:52.730
the role of Bruce Wayne.

00:14:52.730 --> 00:14:56.260
Like Affleck, he's an actor who was perfect
for the role on paper, a leading man on the

00:14:56.260 --> 00:14:58.730
rise who brought an effortless charm to the
role.

00:14:58.730 --> 00:15:02.730
The problem is that "effortless charm" doesn't
really work for Batman—but it ends up working

00:15:02.730 --> 00:15:04.920
pretty well for Bruce Wayne.

00:15:04.920 --> 00:15:08.400
Clooney's best work comes when he's out of
the costume, sweeping around Wayne Manor dealing

00:15:08.400 --> 00:15:12.290
with Alfred's not-quite-fatal illness, or
in the few scenes where we see him as the

00:15:12.290 --> 00:15:16.650
franchise's most convincing version of a celebrity
playboy to date.

00:15:16.650 --> 00:15:20.280
Of all the actors to play Batman, Troy Baker
might be the most versatile.

00:15:20.280 --> 00:15:22.970
He's the only actor to voice both Batman and
the Joker.

00:15:22.970 --> 00:15:27.221
In "Batman vs. Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles,"
he plays them both in the same film, and he's

00:15:27.221 --> 00:15:30.100
even been three different versions of Robin
in other movies.

00:15:30.100 --> 00:15:31.100
He has range.

00:15:31.100 --> 00:15:33.220
He even has range within the same character.

00:15:33.220 --> 00:15:37.180
The version of Batman that he plays in the
kid-targeted "LEGO" movies is a slightly different

00:15:37.180 --> 00:15:40.840
character than we get from the more serious
version in "Batman vs. Teenage Mutant Ninja

00:15:40.840 --> 00:15:41.840
Turtles."

00:15:41.840 --> 00:15:45.190
They're both recognizably Baker, who manages
to carve out a performance that's a little

00:15:45.190 --> 00:15:47.220
more distinct from other voice actors.

00:15:47.220 --> 00:15:51.250
The only real downside is that he often comes
off as a substitute for other actors.

00:15:51.250 --> 00:15:55.470
His Joker, for instance, is very much filling
in for Mark Hamill, and his Batman could be

00:15:55.470 --> 00:15:57.700
seen as a replacement for Kevin Conroy.

00:15:57.700 --> 00:16:00.410
Still, Baker's Batman is incredibly solid.

00:16:00.410 --> 00:16:03.860
He captures the threatening growl of the Dark
Knight while still having enough energy and

00:16:03.860 --> 00:16:06.400
character to keep it from slipping into monotone.

00:16:06.400 --> 00:16:10.790
It's arguable that his performance is what
makes "Batman vs. TMNT" work far better than

00:16:10.790 --> 00:16:13.060
it has any right to.

00:16:13.060 --> 00:16:17.060
Rino Romano's tenure on the animated show
"The Batman," despite lasting a respectable

00:16:17.060 --> 00:16:21.250
five seasons, will always exist in the shadow
of its predecessor, "Batman: The Animated

00:16:21.250 --> 00:16:23.940
Series," and will never be as fondly remembered.

00:16:23.940 --> 00:16:24.940
That's a shame, too.

00:16:24.940 --> 00:16:29.430
He might not be as definitive as Kevin Conroy,
but Romano is actually very good in the part.

00:16:29.430 --> 00:16:33.360
As a younger Batman, Romano's got a little
more lightness and energy to his performance,

00:16:33.360 --> 00:16:36.730
but still keeps the gravelly menace that comes
along with the cape and cowl.

00:16:36.730 --> 00:16:41.290
Romano's one full-length adventure, the spooky,
made-for-TV "The Batman vs. Dracula," is an

00:16:41.290 --> 00:16:45.280
enjoyable movie in its own right, and even
works as a showcase for the redesigned Penguin

00:16:45.280 --> 00:16:46.280
and Joker.

00:16:46.280 --> 00:16:50.260
Still, for the epic confrontation between
bat-themed good guy and a vampire bad guy,

00:16:50.260 --> 00:16:53.880
it's not quite as good as you want it to be,
especially since that story's been told with

00:16:53.880 --> 00:16:55.070
more panache elsewhere.

00:16:55.070 --> 00:16:58.660
That's Romano's role in a nutshell: if we
didn't have anything to compare it to and

00:16:58.660 --> 00:17:02.540
could judge it entirely on its own merits,
he'd be great.

00:17:02.540 --> 00:17:06.089
Director Matt Reeves took a big chance in
casting an actor many viewers mainly know

00:17:06.089 --> 00:17:08.890
as a "Twilight" vampire, Robert Pattinson.

00:17:08.890 --> 00:17:13.490
But as the months of production went by, the
concept of Pattinson Batman won over fans.

00:17:13.490 --> 00:17:17.360
Pattinson's Bruce Wayne is not only younger-looking
than fans have come to expect from the character,

00:17:17.360 --> 00:17:19.770
but he's also decidedly more awkward around
people.

00:17:19.770 --> 00:17:23.670
In case the Nirvana needle-drops didn't get
the point across, this man is filled with

00:17:23.670 --> 00:17:26.640
angst, regret and a healthy dose of self-loathing.

00:17:26.640 --> 00:17:31.090
He clings to his virtuous vigilante mission
as Batman, referring to himself as a "nocturnal

00:17:31.090 --> 00:17:33.591
animal," determined to clean up the streets.

00:17:33.591 --> 00:17:37.580
In a film heavily influenced by '70s cinema,
will he soar like Brewster McCloud or go off

00:17:37.580 --> 00:17:39.630
the deep end like Travis Bickle?

00:17:39.630 --> 00:17:42.460
As Batman, Pattinson's jaw is the best since
Kilmer.

00:17:42.460 --> 00:17:46.460
But his quirky decisions and physicality feel
more like Michael Keaton, without the Tim

00:17:46.460 --> 00:17:47.540
Burton polish.

00:17:47.540 --> 00:17:51.790
This is a World's Greatest Detective who makes
mistakes, gets teased over the declaration,

00:17:51.790 --> 00:17:53.250
"I'm vengeance."

00:17:53.250 --> 00:17:57.660
…and punctuates a daring escape by deploying
his parachute too quickly and slamming into

00:17:57.660 --> 00:17:58.660
the pavement.

00:17:58.660 --> 00:18:01.130
He's flawed, and that's interesting.

00:18:01.130 --> 00:18:04.930
There's a moment in "Batman Begins" that really
captures the intensity Christian Bale brings

00:18:04.930 --> 00:18:06.180
to his performance as Batman:

00:18:06.180 --> 00:18:08.360
"Where were the other drugs going?"

00:18:08.360 --> 00:18:09.900
"I never knew!

00:18:09.900 --> 00:18:11.430
I don't know!

00:18:11.430 --> 00:18:13.480
I swear to G--"

00:18:13.480 --> 00:18:15.020
"Swear to me!"

00:18:15.020 --> 00:18:19.360
Bale's Batman brings that intensity to everything
he does, showing an emotional range that stretches

00:18:19.360 --> 00:18:23.500
from the righteous fury of Batman to the aimless
pain of the orphaned Bruce Wayne.

00:18:23.500 --> 00:18:26.840
It's a power that's only ever matched when
he goes up against Heath Ledger's Joker, one

00:18:26.840 --> 00:18:28.890
of the best performances in the genre.

00:18:28.890 --> 00:18:32.480
The detective part of the package might be
a little lacking, but the way Bale plays off

00:18:32.480 --> 00:18:36.520
of his costars and paints a picture of a man
torn between using his rage to fuel his mission

00:18:36.520 --> 00:18:39.240
and letting it consume him is genuinely incredible.

00:18:39.240 --> 00:18:42.530
The fact that he does it all while speaking
in a voice that sounds like he's chewing rusty

00:18:42.530 --> 00:18:43.530
nails?

00:18:43.530 --> 00:18:45.770
That's just icing on the cake.

00:18:45.770 --> 00:18:49.290
Unlike every other actor on this list, Will
Friedle doesn't play Bruce Wayne.

00:18:49.290 --> 00:18:53.810
He plays Terry McGinnis, a teenager from a
cyberpunk future who takes over the role in

00:18:53.810 --> 00:18:54.900
"Batman Beyond."

00:18:54.900 --> 00:18:59.150
Along with a killer costume design and the
best version of a retired Bruce Wayne, Friedle's

00:18:59.150 --> 00:19:02.050
performance is one of the biggest reasons
why "Batman Beyond" works.

00:19:02.050 --> 00:19:06.270
The way he switches from Terry's normal, lighter
speaking voice to the growly Batman voice

00:19:06.270 --> 00:19:10.050
that he uses is distinct without ever feeling
like he's going too far over the top with

00:19:10.050 --> 00:19:11.050
it.

00:19:11.050 --> 00:19:14.820
He's also an extremely likable character in
a way that Bruce's Batman never really is.

00:19:14.820 --> 00:19:18.470
Terry seems more like a human being and less
like the peak ideal of human perfection who

00:19:18.470 --> 00:19:20.110
is also very grumpy about it.

00:19:20.110 --> 00:19:24.040
The only thing that's holding him back is
that his only film appearance comes in "Batman

00:19:24.040 --> 00:19:27.860
Beyond: Return of the Joker," which has a
very different tone from the "Batman Beyond"

00:19:27.860 --> 00:19:28.860
TV series.

00:19:28.860 --> 00:19:32.080
It's one of the darkest things that DC has
ever done with their animated properties,

00:19:32.080 --> 00:19:36.260
to the point where some pretty graphic scenes
were heavily edited for its first release.

00:19:36.260 --> 00:19:39.800
That said, it's still a well-done and extremely
engaging movie.

00:19:39.800 --> 00:19:44.810
It's just not a good showcase of what makes
Friedle, and Terry, so good in their roles.

00:19:44.810 --> 00:19:49.030
The Tim Burton Batman movies have an incredible
visual style, with a Gotham City that's hellish

00:19:49.030 --> 00:19:53.370
urban sprawl come to life, full of buildings
with exposed ductwork and piping stacked up

00:19:53.370 --> 00:19:55.200
next to Art Deco sculptures.

00:19:55.200 --> 00:19:59.300
They're also full of great bits of satire,
beautiful shots, and amazingly offbeat casts.

00:19:59.300 --> 00:20:03.559
Whether they're good Batman movies is a point
of debate, and no one really captures that

00:20:03.559 --> 00:20:05.220
disconnect more than Michael Keaton.

00:20:05.220 --> 00:20:09.150
He's actually kind of perfect for the kind
of remote, mysterious weirdo who sleeps upside

00:20:09.150 --> 00:20:11.000
down because he wants to be more like a bat.

00:20:11.000 --> 00:20:13.160
"Now you wanna get nuts?

00:20:13.160 --> 00:20:14.160
Come on!

00:20:14.160 --> 00:20:15.330
Let's get nuts!"

00:20:15.330 --> 00:20:19.070
But there's a disconnect between the stylized
goofiness that Burton explored in his earlier

00:20:19.070 --> 00:20:23.030
films and the '80s action movie aesthetic
that saw Keaton's Batman throwing people off

00:20:23.030 --> 00:20:25.860
buildings and blowing up factories with bombs.

00:20:25.860 --> 00:20:30.010
It's not really portrayed as heroism, but
as a personal quest for vengeance that only

00:20:30.010 --> 00:20:32.210
incidentally winds up saving a city.

00:20:32.210 --> 00:20:35.940
Keaton does well in the role, but his Batman
is missing some key components other versions

00:20:35.940 --> 00:20:37.340
of the character nail.

00:20:37.340 --> 00:20:42.130
An ultra-competent, self-absorbed superhero
who writes heavy metal songs about being a

00:20:42.130 --> 00:20:46.690
sad orphan and is seemingly able to appear
at will as soon as no one's looking?

00:20:46.690 --> 00:20:50.970
The LEGO version of Batman is one of those
rare finds: a parody of something that actually

00:20:50.970 --> 00:20:53.070
works pretty well as the thing itself.

00:20:53.070 --> 00:20:55.010
"Batman is very wise.

00:20:55.010 --> 00:20:58.450
I also have huge pecs and a nine-pack.

00:20:58.450 --> 00:21:01.940
Yeah, I've got an extra ab."

00:21:01.940 --> 00:21:05.910
Will Arnett's Batman only builds things out
of black bricks and Arnett ramps up his naturally

00:21:05.910 --> 00:21:08.370
gravelly voice into a spoof of Christian Bale's.

00:21:08.370 --> 00:21:12.610
At the same time, though, he's very rarely
presented as anything other than extremely

00:21:12.610 --> 00:21:16.980
good at what he does, and he really does act
heroically, even if his heroism occasionally

00:21:16.980 --> 00:21:19.460
means that Han Solo gets eaten by a space
worm.

00:21:19.460 --> 00:21:21.140
Either way, it works.

00:21:21.140 --> 00:21:25.000
While other portrayals might move away from
the inherent silliness of the character, embracing

00:21:25.000 --> 00:21:28.730
it gives the LEGO version a flavor that helps
him rival even the best of his big-screen

00:21:28.730 --> 00:21:30.660
contemporaries.

00:21:30.660 --> 00:21:34.350
The best thing about Adam West's performance
as Batman is that it can mean completely different

00:21:34.350 --> 00:21:36.370
things depending when you watch it.

00:21:36.370 --> 00:21:40.870
As a child, it's easy to buy West's spandex-clad
squareness as completely genuine.

00:21:40.870 --> 00:21:42.610
As an adult, you get the joke.

00:21:42.610 --> 00:21:46.180
The fact that he was able to play the perfect
straight man for a wild cast of villains is

00:21:46.180 --> 00:21:49.890
every bit as astonishing as those surface-level
comic book thrills.

00:21:49.890 --> 00:21:53.920
The 1966 movie released between the first
two seasons of the "Batman" TV series has

00:21:53.920 --> 00:21:56.570
everything, in a very literal sense.

00:21:56.570 --> 00:22:00.940
The arch-villains are four of his greatest
foes brought together as the United Underworld,

00:22:00.940 --> 00:22:04.180
complete with a sequence in which Batman and
Robin identify their enemies through truly

00:22:04.180 --> 00:22:05.750
profound leaps of logic.

00:22:05.750 --> 00:22:07.010
"But wait!

00:22:07.010 --> 00:22:10.559
It happened at sea!

00:22:10.559 --> 00:22:11.559
See?

00:22:11.559 --> 00:22:12.559
C for Catwoman!"

00:22:12.559 --> 00:22:15.140
It also features West using the role of Bruce
Wayne as an attempt to draw his enemies out

00:22:15.140 --> 00:22:19.590
into the open, miraculously turning his carefully
modulated tone into barely controlled rage

00:22:19.590 --> 00:22:22.130
at the threat to his love interest, Miss Kitka.

00:22:22.130 --> 00:22:25.840
And the heartbreak in his eyes when he discovers
that she was Catwoman all along?

00:22:25.840 --> 00:22:27.520
It's a surprisingly powerful moment.

00:22:27.520 --> 00:22:31.910
No actor has ever been as strongly identified
with the role of Batman as Adam West, and

00:22:31.910 --> 00:22:33.720
the movie shows exactly why.

00:22:33.720 --> 00:22:37.570
His performance walks the delicate balance
between full-hearted adventure and witty satire

00:22:37.570 --> 00:22:40.980
that the material does, and the end result
is perfect.

00:22:40.980 --> 00:22:43.240
Yes, this one is a bit of a cheat.

00:22:43.240 --> 00:22:47.940
Kevin Conroy has, after all, been the most
prominent Batman actor for over 25 years,

00:22:47.940 --> 00:22:51.480
making his debut on "Batman: The Animated
Series" and staying in the role in many of

00:22:51.480 --> 00:22:54.630
the DC animated movies and the "Arkham" video
games.

00:22:54.630 --> 00:22:59.100
But there's a good reason: Conroy is an amazing
Batman, and everything that makes him great

00:22:59.100 --> 00:23:01.380
is on display in "Mask of the Phantasm."

00:23:01.380 --> 00:23:04.870
One of the great things about "The Animated
Series" was that it gave us a version of Batman

00:23:04.870 --> 00:23:08.520
that was already fully formed, saving the
origin stories for a handful of villains like

00:23:08.520 --> 00:23:09.620
Two-Face and the Riddler.

00:23:09.620 --> 00:23:13.760
For the film, though, the origin story is
a crucial component, and Conroy's performance

00:23:13.760 --> 00:23:14.760
sells it beautifully.

00:23:14.760 --> 00:23:18.590
The scene when he's standing in front of his
parents' graves, pleading with them to understand

00:23:18.590 --> 00:23:22.520
he may give up being Batman because he's finally
found happiness, might sound ridiculous.

00:23:22.520 --> 00:23:25.030
But in practice, it's devastatingly emotional.

00:23:25.030 --> 00:23:29.320
"I know I made a promise, but I didn't see
this coming.

00:23:29.320 --> 00:23:32.080
I didn't count on being happy."

00:23:32.080 --> 00:23:36.160
When that happiness is taken away and Batman
gives himself over to his mission entirely?

00:23:36.160 --> 00:23:37.390
It's fantastic.

00:23:37.390 --> 00:23:41.050
For a generation of Batman fans, Conroy's
is the voice they hear in their heads when

00:23:41.050 --> 00:23:44.670
they read comics, and that alone should show
just how great he was.

00:23:44.670 --> 00:23:49.059
It takes an incredible amount of talent to
redefine the character, but Conroy did it.

00:23:49.059 --> 00:23:51.700
